        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Review\n",
        "\n",
        "-   What did you learn last time?\n",
        "\n",
        "## Recap from Last Time\n",
        "\n",
        "-   Monday: counting words with `Counter`; bar charts with matplotlib\n",
        "-   Downloaded two novels, removed the Gutenberg headers & footers\n",
        "-   Loaded & cleaned them into lists of words\n",
        "-   Tokens, types, and type–token ratio\n",
        "-   Why a fair TTR comparison needs texts of the same length\n",
        "-   Average word length\n",
        "\n",
        "# Line Charts & Zipf's Law\n",
        "\n",
        "## Another Kind of Chart: the **Line** Graph\n",
        "\n",
        "-   On Monday we made **bar charts**: good for comparing categories (words, authors).\n",
        "-   A **line chart** is good when **order matters** (rank, time).\n",
        "-   Let's use one to look at *every* word in a book at once.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Line Chart: Rank vs. Frequency\n",
        "\n",
        "-   Rank 1 = the most frequent word, rank 2 = the next most frequent, and so on.\n",
        "-   Use one of your own word lists (change `book1_words` to your variable name):\n",
        "\n",
        "``` python\n",
        "from collections import Counter\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
        "\n",
        "book1_freqs = [pair[1] for pair in Counter(book1_words).most_common()]\n",
        "book1_ranks = range(1, len(book1_freqs) + 1)    # 1, 2, 3, ... up to the number of words\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.plot(book1_ranks, book1_freqs)\n",
        "plt.title(\"Rank vs. Frequency\")\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"Rank\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"Frequency\")\n",
        "plt.show()\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "## Line Chart: Rank vs. Frequency\n",
        "\n",
        "-   Hard to see anything, right?\n",
        "    -   Researchers often convert the axes to a **log scale** to make the chart more readable.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Log Scale\n",
        "\n",
        "-   A **log scale** spaces 1, 10, 100, 1000 evenly, so huge and tiny numbers fit on the same chart.\n",
        "-   Add `plt.xscale(\"log\")` and `plt.yscale(\"log\")` before `plt.show()`:\n",
        "\n",
        "``` python\n",
        "plt.plot(book1_ranks, book1_freqs)\n",
        "plt.title(\"Rank vs. Frequency (Log Scale)\")\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"Rank\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"Frequency\")\n",
        "plt.xscale(\"log\")\n",
        "plt.yscale(\"log\")\n",
        "plt.savefig(\"zipf_book1.png\")\n",
        "plt.show()\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "## Zipf's Law\n",
        "\n",
        "-   On a log–log chart, rank vs. frequency comes out as a (roughly) **straight line**.\n",
        "-   This is **Zipf's Law**: the 2nd most common word appears about ½ as often as the 1st, the 3rd about ⅓ as often, and so on.\n",
        "-   It holds for nearly every language and text ever measured.\n",
        "-   Does it hold for your *other* book, too? You'll find out in Explore 7.\n",
        "\n",
        "# Activity: Detective Gutenberg\n",
        "\n",
        "## Detective Gutenberg\n",
        "\n",
        "**Goal:** discover what makes an author's writing style distinctive using the Python tools you already know.\n",
        "\n",
        "-   If you don't remember how to do something, revisit past lectures.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Today, you'll:**\n",
        "\n",
        "-   Investigate affixes, dialogue, and the most common words\n",
        "-   Make charts comparing your two authors\n",
        "-   Write up your verdict\n",
        "\n",
        "-   Open your `gut_detective.qmd` from last time and re-run your code, so your word lists are loaded.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Counting Fairly\n",
        "\n",
        "-   Your two books are probably different lengths.\n",
        "-   200 *-ly* words in a short book is not the same as 200 in a long one!\n",
        "-   So we report **rates**: how many per 1,000 words.\n",
        "\n",
        "``` python\n",
        "rate = count / len(words) * 1000\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "-   Use rates for all of today's comparisons.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Explore 4: Adverbs & Affixes\n",
        "\n",
        "1.  How many words end in **-ly**? What is the rate per 1,000 words?\n",
        "    -   Look at the actual list of words. Are they all adverbs? What else sneaks in?\n",
        "2.  Pick **three other affixes** to investigate (e.g. *un-*, *-ness*, *-ment*, *-ful*).\n",
        "    -   Which author uses each one more?\n",
        "\n",
        "-   Hint: `.startswith()`, `.endswith()`, and a filtered list comprehension.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Explore 5: Dialogue\n",
        "\n",
        "-   How much **dialogue** does each author use?\n",
        "-   Go back to your header-free **lists of lines** from last time, and remove the empty lines (remember *Mansfield Park*?)\n",
        "-   Count how many lines contain an opening quotation mark, and what percentage of all lines that is.\n",
        "-   Check how *your* book marks dialogue! Some use `\"` or `“`, others `‘`:\n",
        "\n",
        "``` python\n",
        "quot_list = ['\"', '“', '‘']\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "-   Why do we skip `’`? (Hint: *don’t*, *Alice’s*)\n",
        "\n",
        "## Explore 6: Most Common Words\n",
        "\n",
        "-   What are the **10 most common content words** in each book?\n",
        "-   Use `Counter` and `.most_common()`, plus the `stopwords` list from Monday.\n",
        "    -   Add to the stopword list if function words are still sneaking in.\n",
        "-   What do the top words tell you about each book? (Characters? Settings? Themes?)\n",
        "\n",
        "## Explore 7: Visualize It\n",
        "\n",
        "1.  Make **one bar chart** that compares your two authors on one measure.\n",
        "    -   e.g. *-ly* words per 1,000 words, or percentage of dialogue lines\n",
        "    -   The labels are your authors, and the heights are your measurements:\n",
        "\n",
        "``` python\n",
        "plt.bar([\"Author 1\", \"Author 2\"], [measure_1, measure_2])\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "## Explore 7: Visualize It\n",
        "\n",
        "2.  Make a **Zipf plot** with **both** books on the **same** chart.\n",
        "    -   Call `plt.plot()` once for each book, giving each a `label`, then add `plt.legend()` before `plt.show()`:\n",
        "\n",
        "``` python\n",
        "plt.plot(book1_ranks, book1_freqs, label=\"Author 1\")\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "-   Give every chart a title and axis labels, and save each one with `plt.savefig()`!\n",
